{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>For when shall I entreat for you.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: if it had said מתי אעתיר לך — 'when shall I entreat for you' — it would have meant 'when shall I pray'; but since it said למתי, 'for when,' meaning 'I am praying for you today that they cease — appoint for me the time,' that is, say to me on which day you want them to cease. End quote. Now Moses, upon leaving Pharaoh's presence, would pray immediately that the plague depart the next day; and so you find in the plague of ערוב (swarms), where he said: הנה אנכי יוצא מעמך והעתרתי אל ה' וסר הערוב מפרעה מעבדיו ומעמו מחר — and it is written: ויצא מעם פרעה ויעתר אל ה', for upon leaving he would pray immediately that the plague depart the next day, and this supports the interpretation of Rashi, of blessed memory. <br><b>And the difficulty with Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, is that he said Moses did not pray until the next day, and said that the למ in למתי is superfluous, like its parallel (Deuteronomy 9:7): למן היום אשר יצאת מארץ מצרים — for had he prayed that very day, when the plague departed the next day, it would have been fitting to say ויעש ה' כדבר משה ממחרת; and since it did not say so, it appears that at the time of his prayer the plague was departing immediately, and since the departure of the plague was not until the next day, he did not pray until the next day." "<b>And behold,</b> the verse proves according to the words of Rashi, for it says: הנה אנכי יוצא מעמך והעתרתי אל ה' — and it did not say והעתרתי אל ה' מחר. This teaches you that as soon as he left, he immediately prayed that the plague of ערוב depart from his servants and his people the next day. For since Pharaoh began with this plague of frogs to mention 'tomorrow' and to extend the time until the next day, Moses too agreed in the other plagues to extend their time until the next day, rather than that they depart immediately. From this you find written concerning the plague of ערוב: למחר יהיה האות הזה, and concerning the plague of hail it is written (Exodus 9:18): הנני ממטיר כעת מחר ברד כבד מאד, and concerning the plague of pestilence it is written (ibid.): מחר יעשה ה' הדבר הזה בארץ, and concerning the plague of locusts it is written (ibid. 10): הנני מביא מחר. And the reason Pharaoh extended the first plague until the next day was that he thought the time for the plague's removal had arrived by the way of the array of the stars, and Moses, who knew the time, would request that Pharaoh tell him to remove them immediately; therefore Pharaoh wished to confound his mind, to outwit Moses by his wisdom, and he extended the time until the next day. And Moses our master answered him and said: כדברך — as you say, they shall not depart today until tomorrow. And from this you can understand that the plague is not by the force of the celestial array but by the power of the Omnipresent; this is the meaning of למען תדע כי אין כה' אלהינו."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:07.336827815Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}